WHICH reminds me: doesn't it seem strange to anyone
else that metal armor should *protect* from
_electrical_ attacks?

Idea:

I'd rather see each item of worn metal armor
_increase_ the success rate of electric attacks and
increase the damage rate from their shocking, what
with metal armor being conductive and so getting the
PC zapped badly and also creating more points at
which the PC can be attacked with an electrical
shock.

That change sure would increase the fear factor of
grid bugs in the early game, and of shocking
spheres, blue dragons, monster-held wands of
lightning [and of what else?] later on in the game.

  Apropos of nothing, my PC just got lunched in
  a deep version of Sokoban (DLVL 9 entrance,
  IIRC) by a gnome with a wand of fire. I typed
  too fast, so the gnome got two shots and all my PC
  got was a spiffy decorative tombstone. Ouch! The
  story of my NetHack-playing career, that one is.

_Enchantment_ on metal armor should work the
*opposite* way, as being magical and thus
"everything proofing", and the effects should
counteract one another appropriately. For example, 6
base points of AC defense improvement of plate mail
would give +6 points of electrical attack enhanced
vulnerability, but enchanting the plate mail to +5
points of magical enchantment gives -5 points of
electrical attack enhanced vulnerability, summing
the two gives the final result as +1 point of
electrical attack enhanced vulnerability (and damage
factor increase, how both factors are applied in the
game is deliberately poorly specified).

  Blessing the armor might halve the bad effect,
  cursing the armor might double the bad effect.

I'd mark this as a YANI, but it's so obvious, I'm
sure it's been said often before.

I'd just _love_ to see more NetHack motivations to
use non-metallic armor (and for this purpose, silver
dragon scale mail would count as "metallic"); those
elven leather helmets are way stylish. Hmm, and that
gives me an idea.

